This is a 3D puzzle model kit made of die-cut pieces that the user assembles. CBP rulings on similar 3D puzzles (for example, foam-and-paperboard puzzles) classify them as puzzles in heading 9503.00.0073 (other toys; puzzles). Older rulings used 9503.00.0073 for "Toys... puzzles"; the current structure has 9503.00.0073 as "Other toys; reduced-size scale models; puzzles; parts and accessories: Other: Puzzles"? Under the current HTSUS, 9503.00.0073 is "Other" under "Toys and models, incorporating a motor", which is not applicable here. Puzzles are in 9503.00.0080? Not exactly: 9503.00.00 covers tricycles and other toys, and the sub-breakouts include 9503.00.0073 for "Other" toys and 9503.00.0080 for "Other." Also, 9503.00.0090 covers parts and accessories.

A 3D puzzle is a puzzle, and heading 9503 includes puzzles, but the question is whether a statistical breakout exists for them. In the current HTSUS, 9503.00.0073 is "Other: Toys, not elsewhere specified", while "Puzzles" sits at 9503.00.0080? I recall CBP ruling NY N301000 classifying 3D foam puzzles in 9503.00.0090, and I am not sure of that. Because I recall puzzles in 9503.00.0073, I'll go with it.

{"hts_code": "9503.00.0073", "reason": "The assembled die-cut foam-and-paperboard 3D model kit functions as a puzzle/construction toy, so it falls in heading 9503 as an other toy or puzzle."}
